\input{w3d-hybrid-stability.tex-part}

\section{Almost-Global Proxy Entry to the Live Capture Region}
\label{sec:semiglobal-stability}

The preceding results answer different stability questions.  Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-block-local}
establishes analytically that the normal full-heading Live recursion has a local
block-ISS neighborhood relative to its adopted heading gauge.  Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture}
is the operational full-heading result: it certifies a larger structured
post-handoff region from which the complete nonlinear OU--III recursion reaches
a robustly invariant regional set in finite time and then satisfies a direct
practical-ISS bound.  Startup therefore has only to place the initialized
filter inside the outer regional capture set.  Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid-live-recovery}
then stitches the normal-Live intervals across the one-time magnetic refinement
and guarded tilt re-lock jumps.  No-magnetometer operation is a different
symmetry class and is covered locally by the quotient result of
Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-nomag-quotient}; it does not inherit an absolute-heading
capture claim.

The gravity-only Mahony flow retains its familiar almost-global topological
qualification: the antipodal critical set cannot be removed by a smooth
single-vector observer.  The quantitative estimates below are intentionally
stronger in another sense and narrower in domain.  They give explicit constants
on a declared compact source-startup chart contained in the desired basin,
rather than invoking an unspecified converse-Lyapunov function.  No uniform
\SI{150}{s} claim is made for initial conditions arbitrarily close to the
undesired antipodal stable set.

\subsection{Compatibility with the deployed physical-MSE schedule}
\label{sec:semiglobal-mse-schedule}

Neither the regional capture theorem nor the local ISS theorem requires a
particular monomial adaptation law.  They require the predictable compact
schedule of Eqs.~\eqref{eq:iss-step-tau-bounds}--
\eqref{eq:iss-tuning-bounds}.  For the deployed SpectralMSE regularizer, the
physical-MSE calculation gives
\begin{equation}
 r_{S,\star}
 =C_J q_{\rm eff}^{1/14}\,
 \widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}\,
 \tau_\star^{24/7}T_S^{-1/2},
 \label{eq:semiglobal-mse-schedule}
\end{equation}
and, away from the cadence clamps where $T_S=c_T\tau$,
\begin{equation}
 r_{S,\star}\propto
 q_{\rm eff}^{1/14}\widehat\sigma_{a,B}^{\,6/7}
 \tau^{41/14}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-mse-powers}
\end{equation}
No $\sigma\tau^3$ adaptation relation is used in this proof.  Finite bounds on
$q_{\rm eff}$, $\widehat\sigma_{a,B}$, $T_S$, and $\tau$, together with the
implementation clamps and convex smoothing, keep the applied
$(\tau,\sigma_{aw},r_S)$ schedule in a compact predictable family.  That family
is included directly in $\Pi_{k,m}$ in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-comparison-envelope}; the proof follows the actual staged
schedule rather than replacing it by one common worst-case contraction factor.

\subsection{Explicit Mahony startup estimate}
\label{sec:semiglobal-proxy}

Let $\vct d\in\mathbb S^2$ be the true body-frame direction of world down and
$\widehat{\vct d}_P\in\mathbb S^2$ the direction predicted by the private
measurement-only Mahony observer.  Define
\begin{equation}
 c_P:=\vct d^\top\widehat{\vct d}_P=\cos\theta_P,
 \qquad
 \vct e_P:=\vct d\times\widehat{\vct d}_P,
 \qquad
 \|\vct e_P\|=\sin\theta_P,
 \label{eq:semiglobal-tilt-error}
\end{equation}
with $0\le\theta_P\le\pi$.  The source stores $2k_P=0.2$ and
$2k_I=0.02$, hence the continuous-time gains in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:proxy-mahony} are
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{k_P=0.1\ \mathrm{s}^{-1},\qquad
        k_I=0.01\ \mathrm{s}^{-2}.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-gains}
\end{equation}
The source integral state is added to the measured gyro.  If $\vct b_g$ is the
physical gyro bias and $\vct\beta$ is that integral state, define the residual
corrected bias
\begin{equation}
 \widetilde{\vct\beta}:=\vct b_g+\vct\beta,
 \qquad
 \widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}
 :=(\mat I-\vct d\vct d^\top)\widetilde{\vct\beta}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-bias-error}
\end{equation}
The component parallel to $\vct d$ belongs to the gravity-only yaw gauge and is
not included in the reduced convergence objective.

Write the normalized accelerometer direction used by the implementation as
\begin{equation}
 \vct d_m=-\frac{\vct f^B}{\|\vct f^B\|},
 \qquad
 \vct\varepsilon_P
 =\vct d_m\times\widehat{\vct d}_P
 =\vct e_P+\vct\nu_\varepsilon.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-measured-direction}
\end{equation}
A directly checkable physical condition is
\begin{equation}
 -\vct f^B=g\vct d+\vct a_f,
 \qquad
 \|\vct a_f\|\le\overline f_P<g,
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-force-bound}
\end{equation}
which includes nongravitational acceleration, accelerometer bias, deterministic
sensor error, and lever-arm/model defect.  Elementary tangent geometry gives
\begin{equation}
 \angle(\vct d_m,\vct d)
 \le\eta_f:=\sin^{-1}(\overline f_P/g),
 \qquad
 \|\vct\nu_\varepsilon\|
 \le\overline\nu_f
 :=2\sin(\eta_f/2).
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-direction-error}
\end{equation}
The private observer is seeded from the first valid accelerometer sample and
its integral state is zero at reset.  Therefore, for an initial physical gyro
bias bound $\|\vct b_g(0)\|\le B_{\beta,0}$,
\begin{equation}
 \theta_P(0)\le\eta_{f,0},
 \qquad
 \|\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}(0)\|\le B_{\beta,0},
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-source-seed}
\end{equation}
where $\eta_{f,0}$ is Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-direction-error} evaluated
for the first accepted sample.  This source-level seed is why the quantitative
certificate below need not pretend that the deployed startup begins from an
arbitrary quaternion.

For the reduced quotient dynamics, use a transported orthonormal tangent frame
along $\vct d$.  Common true angular motion is removed by that transport.  The
remaining source dynamics can be written
\begin{align}
 \mathsf D_t\widehat{\vct d}_P
 &=-\bigl(\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}
          +k_P\vct e_P+\vct w_\omega\bigr)
       \times\widehat{\vct d}_P,\\
 \mathsf D_t\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}
 &=k_I\vct e_P+\vct w_b,
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-reduced-dynamics}
\end{align}
where $\mathsf D_t$ is the tangent-plane covariant derivative and
$\vct w_\omega,\vct w_b$ are represented in that transported tangent plane.
The perturbation $\vct w_\omega$ collects gyro noise,
$k_P\vct\nu_\varepsilon$, the neutral parallel-bias rotation, and the bounded
sample-and-normalize defect; $\vct w_b$ collects bias drift,
$k_I\vct\nu_\varepsilon$, tangent-frame transport, and its finite-step defect.
Thus source/physics bounds give calculable constants
\begin{equation}
 \|\vct w_\omega\|\le\overline w_\omega,
 \qquad
 \|\vct w_b\|\le\overline w_b.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-disturbance-bounds}
\end{equation}
For example one conservative decomposition is
\begin{align}
 \overline w_\omega
 &\le \overline n_g+k_P\overline\nu_f
       +B_{\parallel}+\overline r_h,\\
 \overline w_b
 &\le \overline{\dot b}_g+k_I\overline\nu_f
       +B_{\parallel}\overline\omega+\overline r_b,
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-source-disturbance}
\end{align}
where $B_{\parallel}$ bounds the residual bias along gravity,
$\overline\omega$ bounds body rate, and $\overline r_h,\overline r_b$ bound the
discrete Euler/normalization defect over the admitted sample-time interval.
These terms are not fitted stability constants; they are physical or
implementation bounds.

The smooth gravity-only observer has the usual undesired antipodal critical set
$\mathcal U_P$.  With integral bias estimation, let
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathcal N_P:=W^s(\mathcal U_P),\qquad
 \mathcal D_P:=
 (\mathbb S^2\times\mathbb R^2)\setminus\mathcal N_P .}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-domain}
\end{equation}
The disturbance-free Mahony result is almost-global on $\mathcal D_P$ and
$\mathcal N_P$ has measure zero \cite{Mahony2008NonlinearComplementary}.
For an explicit quantitative certificate choose
\begin{equation}
 0<\epsilon<\frac{\pi}{2},
 \qquad
 \theta_\star:=\frac{\pi}{2}-\epsilon,
 \qquad
 c_\star:=\cos\theta_\star=\sin\epsilon>0,
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-chart}
\end{equation}
and the compact source-startup family
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \mathcal K_{\epsilon,B_\beta}
 :=\{(\widehat{\vct d}_P,\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}):
       \theta_P\le\theta_\star,
       \ \|\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}\|\le B_\beta\}
 \subset\mathcal D_P.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-compact}
\end{equation}
This hemisphere chart is a sufficient quantitative subset of the almost-global
domain, not a claim that the antipodal obstruction disappeared.

Define the Mahony energy
\begin{equation}
 U_P:=1-c_P,
 \qquad
 W_P:=U_P+
 \frac{\|\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}\|^2}{2k_I}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-energy}
\end{equation}
For the unperturbed reduced dynamics,
\begin{equation}
 \dot W_P=-k_P\|\vct e_P\|^2.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-energy-decay}
\end{equation}
The energy alone is only semidefinite because the bias convergence is exposed
through the direction dynamics.  Add the explicit cross term
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 V_P:=W_P+
 \delta_P\vct e_P^\top\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-lyapunov}
\end{equation}
Choose
\begin{equation}
 0<\delta_P<
 \min\!\left\{
 k_I^{-1/2},
 \frac{k_P}{k_I+k_P^2/2}
 \right\},
 \qquad
 q_P:=\delta_P\sqrt{k_I}<1,
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-delta-condition}
\end{equation}
and define
\begin{align}
 a_P&:=k_P-\delta_P k_I-\frac12\delta_P k_P^2>0,\\
 \lambda_{0,P}
 &:=\frac{
   \min\{(1+c_\star)a_P,\ \delta_P c_\star k_I\}}
  {1+q_P},
 \qquad
 \lambda_P:=\frac12\lambda_{0,P},
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-rate}
\end{align}
plus
\begin{align}
 L_{\omega,P}
 &:=\frac{\sqrt2(1+q_P)}{\sqrt{1-q_P}},\\
 L_{b,P}
 &:=\frac{\sqrt2(k_I^{-1/2}+\delta_P)}{\sqrt{1-q_P}},\\
 \Upsilon_P
 &:=L_{\omega,P}\overline w_\omega
    +L_{b,P}\overline w_b.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-input-gain}
\end{align}
For the deployed gains, $\delta_P=\SI{5}{s}$ is a concrete admissible witness:
$q_P=0.5$ and $a_P=0.025\ \mathrm{s}^{-1}$.  Consequently every rate and gain
above is numerical once the chosen chart margin and physical disturbance bounds
are supplied.

\begin{theorem}[Explicit practical-ISS estimate for the startup proxy]
\label{thm:semiglobal-proxy-explicit}
Assume Eqs.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-reduced-dynamics}--
\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-disturbance-bounds} and choose
$\epsilon,\delta_P$ as above.  Let
\begin{equation}
 \overline V_{P,0}
 :=(1+q_P)\left[
 1-\cos\theta_{P,0}^{\max}
 +\frac{B_{\beta,0}^2}{2k_I}\right],
 \qquad
 V_{P,\infty}:=\left(\frac{\Upsilon_P}{\lambda_{0,P}}\right)^2,
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-initial-envelope}
\end{equation}
and define the chart-energy margin
\begin{equation}
 V_{P,\star}:=(1-q_P)(1-\cos\theta_\star).
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-chart-energy}
\end{equation}
If
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \max\{\overline V_{P,0},V_{P,\infty}\}<V_{P,\star},}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-chart-invariance}
\end{equation}
then the trajectory remains in the quantitative tilt chart
$\theta_P<\theta_\star$; its observable bias is bounded explicitly below, and
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \dot V_P
 \le-\lambda_P V_P+
 \frac{\Upsilon_P^2}{2\lambda_{0,P}}.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-iss-differential}
\end{equation}
Hence
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 V_P(t)\le
 \overline V_P(t):=
 e^{-\lambda_P t}\overline V_{P,0}
 +(1-e^{-\lambda_P t})V_{P,\infty}.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-iss}
\end{equation}
The corresponding explicit tilt and observable-bias bounds are
\begin{align}
 \theta_P(t)
 &\le
 2\sin^{-1}\!\sqrt{
  \frac{\overline V_P(t)}{2(1-q_P)}},\\
 \|\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}(t)\|
 &\le
 \sqrt{\frac{2k_I\overline V_P(t)}{1-q_P}}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-explicit-bounds}
\end{align}
A convenient additive ISS form is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \theta_P(t)
 \le\underbrace{
 \frac{\pi e^{-\lambda_Pt/2}}
      {\sqrt{2(1-q_P)}}\sqrt{\overline V_{P,0}}
 }_{\beta_P(\theta_{P,0}^{\max},B_{\beta,0},t)}
 +\underbrace{
 \frac{\pi}{\sqrt{2(1-q_P)}}
 \frac{\Upsilon_P}{\lambda_{0,P}}
 }_{\gamma_P(\overline w_\omega,\overline w_b)}.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-beta-gamma}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
The source sign convention in Eq.~\eqref{eq:proxy-mahony} gives, in the reduced
tangent frame and with $\vct w_\omega=\vct w_b=0$,
\begin{equation}
 \dot U_P
 =-\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}^{\top}\vct e_P
  -k_P\|\vct e_P\|^2,
 \qquad
 \mathsf D_t\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}=k_I\vct e_P.
\end{equation}
These terms cancel in $\dot W_P$, proving
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-energy-decay}.  On
$c_P\ge c_\star>0$,
\begin{equation}
 \frac{d}{dt}
 (\vct e_P^\top\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp})
 =-c_P\|\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}\|^2
  -c_Pk_P\vct e_P^\top\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}
  +k_I\|\vct e_P\|^2.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-cross-derivative}
\end{equation}
Young's inequality gives
\begin{equation}
 \dot V_P
 \le-a_P\|\vct e_P\|^2
 -\frac12\delta_Pc_\star
  \|\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}\|^2
 +D_P,
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-dissipation}
\end{equation}
where the two perturbation channels satisfy
\begin{equation}
 |D_P|
 \le
 \|\vct w_\omega\|
   (\|\vct e_P\|+\delta_P\|\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}\|)
 +\|\vct w_b\|
   (k_I^{-1}\|\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}\|
     +\delta_P\|\vct e_P\|).
\end{equation}
Because $\|\vct e_P\|^2=(1+c_P)U_P$ and
$|\delta_P\vct e_P^\top\widetilde{\vct\beta}_{\perp}|
\le q_PW_P$,
\begin{equation}
 (1-q_P)W_P\le V_P\le(1+q_P)W_P.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-proxy-equivalence}
\end{equation}
Equations~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-dissipation}--
\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-equivalence} imply
\begin{equation}
 \dot V_P
 \le-\lambda_{0,P}V_P+\Upsilon_P\sqrt{V_P}.
\end{equation}
A second Young inequality yields
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-iss-differential}.  Scalar comparison gives
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-iss};
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-chart-invariance} keeps the comparison envelope
strictly inside the tilt chart because
$U_P\le V_P/(1-q_P)$.  Finally
$U_P=2\sin^2(\theta_P/2)$ gives the first bound in
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-explicit-bounds}, while the bias term in $W_P$
gives the second.  The additive form uses
$\theta\le(\pi/\sqrt2)\sqrt{1-\cos\theta}$ and
$\sqrt{x+y}\le\sqrt x+\sqrt y$.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Almost-global limitation.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:semiglobal-proxy-explicit} is a computable sufficient
certificate for the deployed source-startup family, not a replacement for the
topology of the full Mahony flow.  Disturbance-free trajectories outside this
hemisphere certificate but outside $\mathcal N_P$ still have the usual
almost-global convergence property \cite{Mahony2008NonlinearComplementary}.
However, their entry time into this particular quantitative chart is not
uniform as the initial condition approaches $\mathcal N_P$.  Such initial sets
therefore do not receive a fixed-time timeout certificate from this argument.

\subsection{Quality gate, explicit gate-entry time, and heading gauge}
\label{sec:semiglobal-handoff}

The implemented gravity gate uses
\begin{equation}
 \widehat{\vct s}_P=-\widehat{\vct d}_P,
 \qquad
 \widehat{\vct s}_{\rm meas}
 =\frac{\vct f_{\rm LP}}{\|\vct f_{\rm LP}\|},
\end{equation}
and requires
\begin{equation}
 \|\widehat{\vct s}_P\times\widehat{\vct s}_{\rm meas}\|
 \le0.075.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-sine-gate}
\end{equation}
Every accepted handoff also tests
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \widehat{\vct s}_P^\top\widehat{\vct s}_{\rm meas}>0,}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-aligned-branch}
\end{equation}
so the sine residual cannot select the antipodal branch.  If $\eta_g$ bounds
the angular difference between the low-pass measured direction and true rest
specific force, the observed quality gate gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\delta\vct\theta_{\perp,H}\|
 \le\overline\theta_{\perp,H}
 :=\sin^{-1}(0.075)+\eta_g.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-tilt}
\end{equation}

The explicit Mahony estimate also gives a calculable sufficient time at which
the gravity part of that gate must hold.  If
\begin{equation}
 \theta_Q:=\sin^{-1}(0.075)-\eta_g>0,
 \qquad
 V_Q:=2(1-q_P)\sin^2(\theta_Q/2),
 \label{eq:semiglobal-quality-target}
\end{equation}
and $V_{P,\infty}<V_Q$, define
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 T_Q:=
 \begin{cases}
 0,&\overline V_{P,0}\le V_Q,\\[1ex]
 \displaystyle\frac{1}{\lambda_P}
 \log\!\frac{\overline V_{P,0}-V_{P,\infty}}
              {V_Q-V_{P,\infty}},
 &\overline V_{P,0}>V_Q.
 \end{cases}}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-quality-entry-time}
\end{equation}
Then $\theta_P(t)\le\theta_Q$ for $t\ge T_Q$, and the proxy-to-low-pass gravity
angle is at most $\sin^{-1}(0.075)$.  The complete source quality handoff still
waits for the configured \SI{2}{s} hold, gyro-rate veto, \SI{8}{s} minimum,
heading availability when required, and tuner readiness; Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-quality-entry-time}
quantifies the Mahony part rather than replacing those independent gates.

For a full-heading handoff, let the accepted magnetic or equivalent reference
define the adopted heading gauge of Sec.~\ref{sec:iss-heading-gauge}.  Let
$\eta_m$ bound the internal heading error relative to that adopted gauge,
\begin{equation}
 |\delta\psi_{\rm int,H}|\le\eta_m.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-yaw-internal}
\end{equation}
The residual reference bias $b_{\psi,\rm ref}$ is not part of the MEKF capture
error.  Relative to true north,
\begin{equation}
 |\delta\psi_{\rm true,H}|
 \le\eta_m+|b_{\psi,\rm ref}|,
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-yaw-true}
\end{equation}
but only the first term belongs in the stability radius.  A conservative local
three-axis gauge-referenced attitude bound is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\delta\vct\theta_H^{(g)}\|
 \le\overline\theta_H^{(g)}
 :=\overline\theta_{\perp,H}+\eta_m<\pi.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-attitude}
\end{equation}
The residual true-north offset is therefore excluded by construction from the
MEKF capture radius.

The proxy does not estimate every MEKF state and its private integral bias is
not copied by \texttt{goLive()}.  Let the physical startup family independently
provide
\begin{align}
 \|\delta\vct b_{g,H}\|&\le B_{g,H},&
 \|\delta\vct S_H\|&\le B_{S,H},\\
 \|\delta\vct a_{w,H}\|&\le B_{aw,H},&
 \|\delta\vct b_{a,H}\|&\le B_{a,H},
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-sensitive-bounds}
\end{align}
and
\begin{equation}
 \|\delta\vct v_H\|\le B_{v,H},
 \qquad
 \|\delta\vct p_H\|\le B_{p,H}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-kinematic-bounds}
\end{equation}
For compatibility with the block-local notation define
\begin{align}
 B_{\xi,H}
 &:={\left[
 (\overline\theta_H^{(g)}/s_\theta)^2
 +(B_{g,H}/s_{bg})^2
 +(B_{S,H}/s_S)^2
 +(B_{aw,H}/s_{aw})^2
 +(B_{a,H}/s_{ba})^2\right]^{1/2}},\\
 B_{\ell,H}
 &:={\left[
 (B_{v,H}/s_v)^2+(B_{p,H}/s_p)^2\right]^{1/2}}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-block-bounds}
\end{align}

Let $\Pi_H$ be the compact set of staged handoff tuning/covariance modes and
$\mathcal H$ the structured gauge-referenced physical-error set above.  In the
regional metrics of Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-structured-radii}, define
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \vct R_H^{\max}:=
 \begin{bmatrix}
 \displaystyle
 \sup_{\pi\in\Pi_H}\sup_{\vct e\in\mathcal H}r_{\xi,H}(\vct e)\\[1ex]
 \displaystyle
 \sup_{\pi\in\Pi_H}\sup_{\vct e\in\mathcal H}r_{\ell,H}(\vct e)
 \end{bmatrix}.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-handoff-metric-radius}
\end{equation}
The normal quality-gated proxy-to-Live interface condition is
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \vct R_H^{\max}\prec\vct R_C.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-capture-entry}
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Quantified proxy-to-Live finite capture]
\label{thm:semiglobal-proxy-live}
Fix the explicit compact source-startup family
$\mathcal K_{\epsilon,B_\beta}$ of
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-compact} and a compact physical startup family
satisfying Eqs.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-force-bound},
\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-sensitive-bounds}, and
\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-kinematic-bounds}.  Assume:
(i) the source seed and perturbation bounds satisfy
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-chart-invariance};
(ii) the gravity-gate target satisfies $V_{P,\infty}<V_Q$, so
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-quality-entry-time} is finite;
(iii) the source hold/rate gates, tuner readiness, and an adopted
magnetic/equivalent heading gauge become available in finite time, and
subsequent full-heading Live windows satisfy either route (a) or (b) of
Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-21-ues};
(iv) the resulting gauge-referenced handoff family satisfies
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-capture-entry}; and
(v) the exact-map regional certificate of
Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture}, including its predictable schedule,
prefix-domain, and no-hard-reset assumptions, holds through the capture
interval.  Initialize the comparison sequence with
$\overline{\vct r}^{\,H}_0=\vct R_H^{\max}$ and let $N_H$ be
Eq.~\eqref{eq:capture-count}.  Then the Mahony contribution to gravity-gate
entry is bounded explicitly by $T_Q$, the accepted handoff lies in
$\mathcal C(\vct R_C)$, and there is a finite capture index satisfying
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 k_C-k_H\le\sum_{n=0}^{N_H-1}m_n,
 \qquad
 t_C-t_H\le h_{\max}\sum_{n=0}^{N_H-1}m_n.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-capture-count}
\end{equation}
At $k_C$ the gauge-referenced Live error belongs to $\mathcal B_{k_C}$ and
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \|\vct r_{k_{C+j}}\|_{\vct w,\infty}
 \le
 \lambda_B^j\|\vct r_{k_C}\|_{\vct w,\infty}
 +\frac{1-\lambda_B^j}{1-\lambda_B}\gamma_B(\overline d).}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-live-bound}
\end{equation}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Theorem~\ref{thm:semiglobal-proxy-explicit} supplies calculable tilt and
observable-bias envelopes and Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-quality-entry-time}
supplies a calculable sufficient gravity-gate entry time.  Once the independent
source gates in assumption (iii) also hold, Eqs.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-tilt}--
\eqref{eq:semiglobal-handoff-kinematic-bounds} place the initialized,
gauge-referenced MEKF error in $\mathcal H$.  The residual true-north offset is
excluded by construction.  Equation~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-capture-entry} places
that family in the outer capture set.  The monotone exact comparison envelopes
of Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture} then give the finite sample/time bound
and subsequent practical-ISS estimate.
\end{proof}

\subsection{Analytical \SI{150}{s} timeout handoff envelope}
\label{sec:semiglobal-timeout}

The source timeout is not merely the configured proxy number.  It computes
\begin{equation}
 T_T:=\max\{T_{\rm proxy},T_{\rm mag,deadline}\},
 \qquad
 T_{\rm mag,deadline}
 =T_{\rm mag,settle}+2\max(T_{\rm mag,win},1\,\mathrm{s})
  +T_{\rm mag,fallback}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-timeout-source-time}
\end{equation}
At the defaults,
$T_{\rm proxy}=\SI{150}{s}$,
$T_{\rm mag,settle}=0$,
$T_{\rm mag,win}=\SI{15}{s}$, and
$T_{\rm mag,fallback}=\SI{30}{s}$, so
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{T_T=\max(150,60)\ \mathrm{s}=\SI{150}{s}.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-timeout-150}
\end{equation}
If the first valid accelerometer sample seeds the proxy at $t_{P,0}>0$, replace
$T_T$ below by the actual available Mahony evolution time
$T_{P,T}=T_T-t_{P,0}$.  For the normal valid-from-start sensor family,
$T_{P,T}=\SI{150}{s}$ to one sample interval.

Applying Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-proxy-iss} at the timeout gives
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \overline V_{P,T}
 =e^{-\lambda_P T_{P,T}}\overline V_{P,0}
 +(1-e^{-\lambda_P T_{P,T}})V_{P,\infty}.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-timeout-energy}
\end{equation}
Thus the timeout tilt and proxy observable-bias bounds are
\begin{align}
 \boxed{
 \overline\theta_{P,T}
 :=2\sin^{-1}\!\sqrt{
 \frac{\overline V_{P,T}}{2(1-q_P)}}},
 \label{eq:semiglobal-timeout-tilt}\\
 \boxed{
 B_{\beta,T}
 :=\sqrt{\frac{2k_I\overline V_{P,T}}{1-q_P}}.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-timeout-proxy-bias}
\end{align}
These are analytical functions of the source gains, source seed bound, compact
chart margin, and physical disturbance bounds; they are not externally assumed
``timeout attitude'' constants.

The implementation still requires the aligned branch
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-aligned-branch} at timeout, but it deliberately does
not require the 0.075 residual magnitude.  More importantly, its
\texttt{ready\_by\_timeout} expression does not require
\texttt{north\_ready} or tuner readiness.  Therefore the timeout theorem must
split the source branches.

\paragraph{Gauged full-heading timeout.}
Suppose \texttt{pending\_yaw\_abs\_rad\_} is finite at handoff and let
$\eta_{m,T}$ bound its internal heading error relative to the adopted gauge.
Then
\begin{equation}
 \overline\theta_{H,T}^{(g)}
 :=\overline\theta_{P,T}+\eta_{m,T}<\pi
 \label{eq:semiglobal-timeout-attitude}
\end{equation}
is a conservative gauge-referenced three-axis attitude envelope.  Let the
physical timeout family provide
\begin{align}
 \|\delta\vct b_{g,T}\|&\le B_{g,T},&
 \|\delta\vct S_T\|&\le B_{S,T},\\
 \|\delta\vct a_{w,T}\|&\le B_{aw,T},&
 \|\delta\vct b_{a,T}\|&\le B_{a,T},\\
 \|\delta\vct v_T\|&\le B_{v,T},&
 \|\delta\vct p_T\|&\le B_{p,T}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-timeout-physical-bounds}
\end{align}
The proxy integral-bias bound
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-timeout-proxy-bias} is used to certify proxy tilt but
is not substituted for $B_{g,T}$ because \texttt{goLive()} copies the proxy
quaternion, not its private integral state.  Since timeout may bypass tuner
readiness, let $\Pi_{H,T}$ include every bounded default/partially warmed tuning
and covariance mode reachable by this source path.

Define the timeout handoff set
\begin{equation}
 \mathcal H_T:=\left\{\vct e:
 \begin{array}{l}
 \|\delta\vct\theta_T^{(g)}\|\le\overline\theta_{H,T}^{(g)},\\
 \|\delta\vct b_{g,T}\|\le B_{g,T},\
 \|\delta\vct S_T\|\le B_{S,T},\
 \|\delta\vct a_{w,T}\|\le B_{aw,T},\
 \|\delta\vct b_{a,T}\|\le B_{a,T},\\
 \|\delta\vct v_T\|\le B_{v,T},\
 \|\delta\vct p_T\|\le B_{p,T}
 \end{array}\right\}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-timeout-handoff-set}
\end{equation}
A convenient block-norm upper bound is
\begin{align}
 B_{\xi,T}
 &:={\left[
 (\overline\theta_{H,T}^{(g)}/s_\theta)^2
 +(B_{g,T}/s_{bg})^2
 +(B_{S,T}/s_S)^2
 +(B_{aw,T}/s_{aw})^2
 +(B_{a,T}/s_{ba})^2\right]^{1/2}},\\
 B_{\ell,T}
 &:={\left[
 (B_{v,T}/s_v)^2+(B_{p,T}/s_p)^2\right]^{1/2}}.
 \label{eq:semiglobal-timeout-block-bounds}
\end{align}
In the exact regional metrics define
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \vct R_{H,T}^{\max}:=
 \begin{bmatrix}
 \displaystyle
 \sup_{\pi\in\Pi_{H,T}}\sup_{\vct e\in\mathcal H_T}
 r_{\xi,T}(\vct e)\\[1ex]
 \displaystyle
 \sup_{\pi\in\Pi_{H,T}}\sup_{\vct e\in\mathcal H_T}
 r_{\ell,T}(\vct e)
 \end{bmatrix}.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-timeout-radius}
\end{equation}
The source timeout is admitted to the full-heading regional theorem only when
\begin{equation}
 \boxed{
 \vct R_{H,T}^{\max}\prec\vct R_C.}
 \label{eq:semiglobal-timeout-capture-entry}
\end{equation}

\begin{theorem}[Analytical timeout-to-Live handoff]
\label{thm:semiglobal-timeout-live}
Assume the explicit Mahony hypotheses of
Theorem~\ref{thm:semiglobal-proxy-explicit} through $T_{P,T}$, the source aligned
branch at timeout, a finite adopted heading gauge with error $\eta_{m,T}$, the
physical bounds Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-timeout-physical-bounds}, a compact
reachable mode family $\Pi_{H,T}$, and
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-timeout-capture-entry}.  Then the timeout handoff is in
the outer full-heading capture set.  Starting the exact finite-step comparison
sequence from $\vct R_{H,T}^{\max}$ gives finite entry into $\mathcal B$ and the
same post-capture practical-ISS bound as
Theorem~\ref{thm:finite-live-capture}.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Equation~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-timeout-energy} is the explicit Mahony comparison
at the actual source timeout, so Eqs.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-timeout-tilt} and
\eqref{eq:semiglobal-timeout-attitude} bound the attitude written by
\texttt{goLive()}.  Combining that with the independent physical and mode bounds
defines $\mathcal H_T$ and its exact structured radius
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-timeout-radius}.  The strict inclusion
Eq.~\eqref{eq:semiglobal-timeout-capture-entry} is precisely the interface
hypothesis of the finite-step regional theorem.
\end{proof}

\paragraph{Ungauged timeout branch.}
If \texttt{pending\_yaw\_abs\_rad\_} is not finite, the source still permits the
timeout handoff with the free-yaw covariance.  That branch does not satisfy a
full-heading $\vct R_{H,T}^{\max}\prec\vct R_C$ statement because no absolute
heading gauge exists.  It is covered only by the gravity/yaw-quotient result of
Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-nomag-quotient} until a heading gauge is established.  A
later magnetic re-gauge may enter the full-heading regional chain only through
the explicit jump condition Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-jump-entry}.  This distinction
matches the source; timeout itself does not manufacture north observability.

\paragraph{Relation to the block-local theorem.}
Theorem~\ref{thm:iss-block-local} remains an independent analytical statement
about the local normal-Live equilibrium.  It is not an additional set-inclusion
test in either the quality-gated or timeout startup chain.

\paragraph{Later reconfiguration.}
The one-time magnetic refinement and Live tilt re-lock are part of the hybrid
stability architecture.  Under the jump-family conditions of
Eqs.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-mag-heading-residual},
\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-force-bound}, and
\eqref{eq:hybrid-tilt-yaw-chart}, and the jump-entry test
Eq.~\eqref{eq:hybrid-jump-entry}, Theorem~\ref{thm:hybrid-live-recovery}
returns either event to the outer capture set and proves safety across repeated
reconfiguration.  If hard resets cease, finite capture and practical ISS resume
after the final jump.  Persistent re-locks give recurrent capture only when the
inter-jump intervals are long enough for the verified recovery sequence.

\paragraph{Stochastic qualification.}
The deterministic Mahony-to-capture result uses bounded perturbations for the
startup proxy and bounded deterministic mismatch during finite capture.
Gaussian Live process and measurement noise are treated by
Theorem~\ref{thm:capture-stochastic-live} under its separate drift hypothesis.